      "**B.** → True\n\nThe **converse** reverses the arrow. From $p \\Rightarrow q$ you do not get $q \\Rightarrow p$ for free:\n\n$$q \\Rightarrow p$$\n\nIs a separate claim that can fail even when the original implication holds.\n\n(-1)ⁿ never settles on a single value (it oscillates forever between -1 and 1), so it does not converge, yet it stays within [-1, 1], so it is bounded - Q true (bounded), P false (doesn't converge), breaking $Q \\Rightarrow P$.\n\nThe claim holds on this reading.",
      "**C.** → True\n\nThe **converse** reverses the arrow. From $p \\Rightarrow q$ you do not get $q \\Rightarrow p$ for free:\n\n$$q \\Rightarrow p$$\n\nIs a separate claim that can fail even when the original implication holds.\n\nThe **inverse** $\\neg P \\Rightarrow \\neg Q$ is always equivalent to the converse $Q \\Rightarrow P$.\n\nSince (-1)ⁿ makes the converse false (bounded but not convergent), it makes the inverse false too: $\\neg P$ is true (doesn't converge) but $\\neg Q$ is false (it IS bounded).\n\nSo the claim is true: it lines up with those shared facts\n\nA classic counter-pattern is a true implication with a false converse: every square is a rectangle, but not every rectangle is a square. The same asymmetry is what the claim is trying (and failing) to ignore.\n\nBecause the inverse is logically independent of the original implication, you need a separate reason before accepting it — the given arrow alone is not enough.\n\nSo the statement is True.",
